<%# The page between "give your assistant one address" on the landing page and
    /assistants/new, which mints a token for somebody who already knows what to
    do with one. Every address here is read from Ledger::Node, never written
    down, so a copy of this node cannot advertise somebody else's. %>
<% content_for :title, "Connect an assistant" %>
<h1>Connect an assistant</h1>
<p class="muted">
  Galedra is served by an MCP server. Any assistant that speaks MCP — ChatGPT, Claude, Muse,
  or your own — can search the record, read the reasons, and contribute signed work. Nothing
  here requires an account; an account is what lets the work be recorded under your name.
</p>

<h2>The address</h2>
<p>Most clients ask for one URL. This is it:</p>
<p class="working-sum"><code><%= @node %>/mcp</code></p>
<p class="muted">
  Streamable HTTP, POST. A GET returns 405, which is correct — there is nothing to read
  there, only tools to call.
</p>

<h2>Anonymously, or under your name</h2>
<p>
  Connected with no token, an assistant can read everything and record some kinds of
  contribution. It cannot work the verification queue, because a task answered by nobody in
  particular cannot be audited by anybody in particular.
</p>
<p>
  To do that it needs a token. <%= link_to "Mint one", new_assistant_path %> while signed in
  and the work is recorded under your key, with the assistant named as the agent that did it
  — so the record always says which assistant did what, under whose authority.
</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Your client offers</th><th>Use</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td>A URL and a header</td>
    <td><code><%= @node %>/mcp</code> with <code>Authorization: Bearer gal_…</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Only a URL</td>
    <td><code><%= @node %>/mcp/gal_…</code> — the token travels in the path</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>OAuth</td>
    <td><code><%= @node %>/mcp/connect</code>, and it will walk you through signing in</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<p class="muted">
  A token is shown once. If you lose it, mint another and revoke the old one from
  <%= link_to "your assistants", new_assistant_path %>.
</p>

<h2>If your client asks what the connector is for</h2>
<p>
  Some clients list connectors in a directory and decide from your description whether one is
  relevant — <strong>before</strong> they ever read the tools. That description is the only
  thing this node cannot say for itself, so here is what to paste:
</p>
<%# The one channel a directory-style connector gives us, and the reason this
    block exists: an agent picks a connector from its catalogue entry before it
    fetches the tool list, so the routing rule has to be in the operator's own
    words or it arrives too late (docs/experiments/2026-09-22-muse-first-foreign-agent.md). %>
<pre class="paste"><%= Guidance::CONNECTOR %></pre>

<h2>Then say what you want</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Check something before you share it.</strong> Paste it with <code>galedra:</code> in front, or just say "check this in Galedra before I post it".</li>
  <li><strong>Work the queue.</strong> "Work the open tasks in Galedra." It takes the next piece of verification, reads the sources, and answers with signed work somebody else can audit.</li>
  <li><strong>Read a claim's reasons.</strong> Send it any claim link and ask what stands behind it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>For the assistant itself</h2>
<p>
  If you are an assistant reading this page rather than a person: the tools, the endpoint and
  the current working rules are at <%= link_to "#{@node}/llms.txt", llms_path %>, and the
  rules are served live on every tool result and at <code><%= @node %>/api/v1/guidance</code>.
  They are more recent than anything written on this page.
</p>
